[{"location":"api/#FiberNlse","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.NoRaman","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.NoRaman","text":"NoRaman\n\nAn empty RamanModel struct to avoid simulating the Raman effect.\n\nSee also\n\nRamanModel\nWaveguide\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"constant"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.GNLSEProblem","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.GNLSEProblem","text":"GNLSEProblem\n\nStruct containing all the parameters and precomputed arrays needed for solving the generalized nonlinear Schrödinger equation (GNLSE).\n\nFields\n\nω::AbstractArray{Float64}: Frequency grid\ndt::Float64: Time step\nN::Int: Number of time/frequency points\nfftp::Any: FFT plan\nifftp::Any: IFFT plan\ndispersion_term::AbstractArray{ComplexF64}: Precomputed dispersion term\nnonlinear_function::Function: Nonlinear operator function\nfr::Float64: Fractional Raman contribution\nγ::Float64: Nonlinear coefficient\nraman_freq_response::Union{Nothing, AbstractArray{ComplexF64}}: Raman frequency response (if any)\nω0::Float64: Central frequency\nL::Float64: Waveguide length\n\nSee also\n\nWaveguide\ngnlse\nSolution\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.GNLSEProblem-Tuple{AbstractArray{Float64}, Waveguide}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.GNLSEProblem","text":"GNLSEProblem(t::AbstractArray{Float64}, wg::Waveguide)\n\nConstructs a GNLSEProblem struct containing all parameters and precomputed arrays needed for solving the generalized nonlinear Schrödinger equation (GNLSE) for a given time grid t and Waveguide wg.\n\nSee also\n\nWaveguide\ngnlse\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.RamanModel","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.RamanModel","text":"RamanModel\n\nStruct describing the Raman response of the waveguide.\n\nFields\n\nfr::Float64: Fractional contribution of the Raman effect to the overall nonlinear effect.\ntime_response::Function: User-defined function returning the Raman impulse response as a function of time.\n\nSee also\n\nraman_linagrawaal\nWaveguide\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.Solution","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.Solution","text":"Solution\n\nStruct containing the result of the simulation.\n\nFields\n\nz::AbstractArray{Float64}: Distance vector\nt::AbstractArray{Float64}: Time vector\nf::AbstractArray{Float64}: Frequency vector\nAt::Matrix{ComplexF64}: Time-domain propagation field\nAf::Matrix{ComplexF64}: Frequency-domain (spectrum) propagation field\n\nSee also\n\ncombine\nGNLSEProblem\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.Stepper","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.Stepper","text":"Stepper\n\nStruct containing the fields that are updated or needed during the solver iterations.\n\nFields\n\nU::AbstractArray{ComplexF64}: Current field (frequency domain)\nNU::AbstractArray{ComplexF64}: Nonlinear operator applied to field\ndz::Float64: Current step size\nz::Float64: Current propagation distance\nlocal_error::Float64: Local error estimate\nk1, k2, k3, k4, k5: Runge-Kutta intermediate steps\nUip, U1, U2: Intermediate fields\ne, r: Temporary arrays for computation\nit::Int: Iteration counter\n\nSee also\n\nGNLSEProblem\ngnlse\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.Waveguide","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.Waveguide","text":"Waveguide\n\nStruct describing the waveguide, i.e., the propagation conditions.\n\nFields\n\nα::Union{Float64, Array{Float64}}: Linear optical intensity loss factor (scalar or frequency dependent)\nβs::Array{Float64}: GVD orders vector (starting at 2nd order)\nγ::Union{Float64, Array{Float64}}: Nonlinear factor (scalar or frequency dependent)\nλc::Float64: Center wavelength of the problem (signal and dispersion)\nL::Float64: Physical length of the waveguide\nraman_model::RamanModel: Raman model of the waveguide (RamanModel)\nself_steepening::Bool: Whether to take into account the self-steepening effect\n\nSee also\n\nRamanModel\nGNLSEProblem\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.Waveguide-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.Waveguide","text":"Waveguide(α, βs, γ, λc, L; raman_model=NoRaman, self_steepening=false)\n\nConstructs a Waveguide struct.\n\nBy default, no Raman effect nor self-steepening is activated.\n\nSee also\n\nWaveguide\nRamanModel\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse._compute_error!-Tuple{FiberNlse.Stepper, Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse._compute_error!","text":"_compute_error!(stepper::Stepper, a, b)\n\nUpdates the local_error field of stepper with the normalized root mean square error between arrays a and b.\n\nSee also\n\n_compute_error\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse._compute_error-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse._compute_error","text":"_compute_error(a, b)\n\nComputes the normalized root mean square error between two arrays a and b.\n\nSee also\n\n_compute_error!\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse._erk4ip_step!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse._erk4ip_step!","text":"_erk4ip_step!(stepper, model)\n\nPerform a single embedded Runge-Kutta 4(5) IP step for the GNLSE integration.\n\nThis function updates the fields in stepper in-place using the current model parameters, including the nonlinear operator and dispersion term.\n\nArguments\n\nstepper::Stepper: The stepper struct holding the current state and intermediate arrays.\nmodel::GNLSEProblem: The problem definition containing all precomputed arrays and parameters.\n\nModifies\n\nUpdates all relevant fields of stepper in-place for the next integration step.\n\nReferences\n\nHult, J. (2007). \"A Fourth-Order Runge–Kutta in the Interaction Picture Method for Simulating Supercontinuum Generation in Optical Fibers.\" J. Lightwave Technol. 25, 3770-3775. doi:10.1109/JLT.2007.909373\n\nSee also\n\nStepper\nGNLSEProblem\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse._integrate_to_z-Tuple{FiberNlse.Stepper, Float64, GNLSEProblem, Int64, Float64}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse._integrate_to_z","text":"_integrate_to_z(stepper::Stepper, z::Float64, prob::GNLSEProblem, maxiters::Int, reltol::Float64)\n\nIntegrates the GNLSE from the current position of stepper up to distance z, using adaptive step size control. Throws an error if maxiters is exceeded.\n\nSee also\n\ngnlse\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse._spm-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse._spm","text":"_spm(u, model)\n\nNonlinear operator for self-phase modulation (SPM) only (no Raman, no self-steepening).\n\nArguments\n\nu: Input field (time domain).\nmodel: GNLSEProblem or model struct with parameters.\n\nReturns\n\nNonlinear term in frequency domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse._spm_raman-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse._spm_raman","text":"_spm_raman(u, model)\n\nNonlinear operator for SPM with Raman effect (no self-steepening).\n\nArguments\n\nu: Input field (time domain).\nmodel: GNLSEProblem or model struct with parameters.\n\nReturns\n\nNonlinear term in frequency domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse._spm_self_steepening-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse._spm_self_steepening","text":"_spm_self_steepening(u, model)\n\nNonlinear operator for SPM with self-steepening (no Raman).\n\nArguments\n\nu: Input field (time domain).\nmodel: GNLSEProblem or model struct with parameters.\n\nReturns\n\nNonlinear term in frequency domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse._spm_self_steepening_raman-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse._spm_self_steepening_raman","text":"_spm_self_steepening_raman(u, model)\n\nNonlinear operator for SPM with self-steepening and Raman effect.\n\nArguments\n\nu: Input field (time domain).\nmodel: GNLSEProblem or model struct with parameters.\n\nReturns\n\nNonlinear term in frequency domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.choose_nonlinear_term","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.choose_nonlinear_term","text":"choose_nonlinear_term(self_steepening::Bool = true, raman::Bool = true)\n\nSelect the appropriate nonlinear operator function based on self-steepening and Raman effect flags.\n\nArguments\n\nself_steepening::Bool: Whether to include self-steepening.\nraman::Bool: Whether to include Raman effect.\n\nReturns\n\nFunction: The selected nonlinear operator function.\n\nSee also\n\n_spm\n_spm_self_steepening\n_spm_self_steepening_raman\n_spm_raman\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.combine-Tuple{Solution, Solution}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.combine","text":"Combines two different Solution structs to an unique one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.combine-Tuple{Vector{Solution}}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.combine","text":"Combines a set of Solution structs to an unique one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.gnlse-Tuple{AbstractArray{ComplexF64}, AbstractArray{Float64}, GNLSEProblem}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.gnlse","text":"gnlse(u::AbstractArray{ComplexF64}, t::AbstractArray{Float64}, prob::GNLSEProblem; nsaves=20, dz=1.0, reltol=1e-6, maxiters=1000)\n\nSolves the GNLSE for initial field u and time grid t using the problem definition prob. Returns a Solution struct with the propagation results.\n\nSee also\n\nGNLSEProblem\nSolution\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.gnlse-Tuple{AbstractArray{ComplexF64}, AbstractArray{Float64}, Vector{GNLSEProblem}}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.gnlse","text":"gnlse(u::AbstractArray{ComplexF64}, t::AbstractArray{Float64}, probs::Vector{GNLSEProblem}; args...)\n\nPropagates the field u through a sequence of GNLSEProblems, chaining the output of each as the input to the next. Returns a combined Solution.\n\nSee also\n\ncombine\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.gnlse-Tuple{AbstractArray{ComplexF64}, AbstractArray{Float64}, Vector{Waveguide}}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.gnlse","text":"gnlse(u::AbstractArray{ComplexF64}, t::AbstractArray{Float64}, wgs::Vector{Waveguide}; args...)\n\nPropagates the field u through a sequence of Waveguides, constructing a GNLSEProblem for each. Returns a combined Solution.\n\nSee also\n\nGNLSEProblem\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.gnlse-Tuple{AbstractArray{ComplexF64}, AbstractArray{Float64}, Waveguide}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.gnlse","text":"gnlse(u::AbstractArray{ComplexF64}, t::AbstractArray{Float64}, wg::Waveguide; args...)\n\nConvenience wrapper for gnlse that constructs a GNLSEProblem from a Waveguide and calls gnlse.\n\nSee also\n\nGNLSEProblem\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.input-Tuple{Solution}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.input","text":"Returns the time and frequency domain input signal of a Solution\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.output-Tuple{Solution}","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.output","text":"Returns the time and frequency domain output signal of a Solution\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#FiberNlse.raman_linagrawaal","page":"API","title":"FiberNlse.raman_linagrawaal","text":"Agrawaal Raman model\n\nDefault values are (fr = 0.18 , τl = 32e-15 [s], τvib = 12.2e-15 [s])\n\nConstruct the Agrawal Raman model as a RamanModel with typical silica fiber parameters.\n\nArguments\n\nfr: Fractional Raman contribution (default: 0.18)\nτl: Raman longitudinal relaxation time in seconds (default: 32e-15)\nτvib: Raman vibrational period in seconds (default: 12.2e-15)\n\nReturns\n\nRamanModel: A RamanModel struct with the Agrawal time response.\n\nReferences\n\nG. P. Agrawal, Nonlinear Fiber Optics, 5th Edition, Eq. (7.1.3)\n\nSee also\n\nRamanModel\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"theory/#Derivation-of-the-Nonlinear-Schrödinger-Equation-(NLSE)","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"The propagation of an optical pulse in a nonlinear and dispersive medium can be described starting from Maxwell's equations. Here, we outline the derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE) using the Slowly Varying Envelope Approximation (SVEA), following the approach in G. P. Agrawal's Nonlinear Fiber Optics.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#1.-Starting-from-Maxwell's-Equations","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"1. Starting from Maxwell's Equations","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"The wave equation for the electric field mathbfE in a dielectric medium is:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"nabla^2 mathbfE - mu_0 epsilon_0 fracpartial^2 mathbfEpartial t^2 = mu_0 fracpartial^2 mathbfPpartial t^2","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where mathbfP = mathbfP_textL + mathbfP_textNL is the total polarization, including both linear and nonlinear contributions.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#2.-Linear-and-Nonlinear-Polarization","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"2. Linear and Nonlinear Polarization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"Linear polarization: mathbfP_textL = epsilon_0 chi^(1) mathbfE\nNonlinear polarization: For Kerr media, mathbfP_textNL = epsilon_0 chi^(3) mathbfE^2 mathbfE","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#3.-The-Slowly-Varying-Envelope-Approximation-(SVEA)","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"3. The Slowly Varying Envelope Approximation (SVEA)","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"Assume the electric field can be written as:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"mathbfE(z t) = frac12 left A(z t) e^i(beta_0 z - omega_0 t) + textcc right","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where A(z t) is the slowly varying envelope, omega_0 is the carrier frequency, and beta_0 = beta(omega_0).","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"The polarization can be similarly written as:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"mathbfP(z t) = frac12 left P(z t) e^i(beta_0 z - omega_0 t) + textcc right","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where P(z t) is the envelope of the polarization.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#4.-Substituting-into-the-Wave-Equation","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"4. Substituting into the Wave Equation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"Substitute the expressions for mathbfE(z t) and mathbfP(z t) into the wave equation. After some algebra and using the SVEA (neglecting partial^2 Apartial z^2), the equation for the envelope becomes:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"left( fracpartialpartial z + i beta_0 right)^2 A - frac1c^2 left( fracpartialpartial t - i omega_0 right)^2 A = -mu_0 fracpartial^2 Ppartial t^2 e^-i(beta_0 z - omega_0 t)","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"Expanding and keeping only first-order derivatives in z (SVEA), and transforming to the frequency domain, we get:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"fracpartial Apartial z + i left beta(omega) - beta_0 right A = i fracomega_0^22 beta_0 c^2 P_textNL","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where P_textNL is the nonlinear polarization envelope.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#5.-Including-Dispersion","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"5. Including Dispersion","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"Expand the propagation constant beta(omega) in a Taylor series around omega_0 to arbitrary order:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"beta(omega) = beta_0 + sum_n=1^infty frac1n beta_n (omega - omega_0)^n","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"beta_n = left fracd^n betadomega^n right_omega_0","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"is the n-th order dispersion coefficient.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"Transforming back to the time domain, the dispersion operator acts as:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"sum_n=1^infty fraci^n-1n beta_n fracpartial^npartial t^n A","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#6.-Nonlinear-Polarization-and-the-Nonlinear-Parameter","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"6. Nonlinear Polarization and the Nonlinear Parameter","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"For a Kerr medium, the nonlinear polarization envelope is:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"P_textNL(z t) = epsilon_0 chi^(3) A(z t)^2 A(z t)","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"The nonlinear parameter is defined as:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"gamma = fracomega_0 n_2c A_texteff","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where n_2 is the nonlinear refractive index and A_texteff is the effective mode area.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#7.-The-Generalized-Nonlinear-Schrödinger-Equation","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"7. The Generalized Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"Combining the above, the envelope A(z t) satisfies:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"fracpartial Apartial z + sum_n=1^infty fraci^n-1n beta_n fracpartial^n Apartial t^n = i gamma A^2 A","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"beta_n are the dispersion coefficients,\ngamma is the nonlinear parameter.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#8.-Moving-to-the-Retarded-Time-Frame","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"8. Moving to the Retarded Time Frame","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"In a reference frame moving at the group velocity (tau = t - zv_g, with v_g = 1beta_1), the equation becomes:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"fracpartial Apartial z + sum_n=2^infty fraci^n-1n beta_n fracpartial^n Apartial tau^n = i gamma A^2 A","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where the sum starts from n=2 since the first-order term is eliminated in the moving frame.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"This is the generalized form of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE) for pulse propagation in a dispersive and nonlinear medium, including arbitrary high-order dispersion.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#9.-Including-the-Raman-Effect","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"9. Including the Raman Effect","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"In real optical fibers, the nonlinear response is not purely instantaneous. The delayed Raman response can be included by modifying the nonlinear term to account for both instantaneous (electronic) and delayed (Raman) contributions. The nonlinear polarization envelope becomes:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"P_textNL(z t) = epsilon_0 chi^(3) left (1 - f_R) A(z t)^2 A(z t) + f_R A(z t) int_-infty^infty h_R(t) A(z t - t)^2 dt right","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"f_R is the fractional contribution of the delayed Raman response (typically f_R approx 018 for silica),\nh_R(t) is the Raman response function.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"The generalized NLSE including the Raman effect is:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"fracpartial Apartial z + sum_n=2^infty fraci^n-1n beta_n fracpartial^n Apartial tau^n = i gamma left (1 - f_R) A^2 A + f_R A int_-infty^infty h_R(tau) A(tau - tau)^2 dtau right","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"This equation describes pulse propagation in a nonlinear dispersive medium, including arbitrary high-order dispersion and the delayed Raman response.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/#10.-Including-Self-Steepening","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"10. Including Self-Steepening","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"Self-steepening is a higher-order nonlinear effect that arises due to the intensity dependence of the group velocity, leading to an asymmetric spectral broadening of ultrashort pulses. It can be incorporated by adding a term that accounts for the time derivative of the nonlinear response. The generalized NLSE including both Raman effect and self-steepening becomes:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"fracpartial Apartial z + sum_n=2^infty fraci^n-1n beta_n fracpartial^n Apartial tau^n =\ni gamma left 1 + fraciomega_0 fracpartialpartial tau right left( (1 - f_R) A^2 A + f_R A int_-infty^infty h_R(tau) A(tau - tau)^2 dtau right)","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"where the operator 1 + fraciomega_0 fracpartialpartial tau accounts for self-steepening, and omega_0 is the carrier frequency.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"This equation describes pulse propagation in a nonlinear dispersive medium, including arbitrary high-order dispersion, the delayed Raman response, and self-steepening.","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"References:","category":"page"},{"location":"theory/","page":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","title":"Derivation of the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE)","text":"G. P. Agrawal, Nonlinear Fiber Optics, 5th Edition, Academic Press, 2013.","category":"page"},{"location":"#FiberNlse","page":"Home","title":"FiberNlse","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Documentation for FiberNlse.","category":"page"},{"location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","title":"Documentation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Theoretical background","category":"page"},{"location":"#Index","page":"Home","title":"Index","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"","category":"page"}]
